threadpool: shed blame load with 503 instead of cosmetic overload flag

From: Andy Green <andy@warmcat.com>

When a blame URL was detected with the pool already busy, the code only
set priv->blame_overloaded, which just ORs a bit (JG2_CTX_FLAG_BLAME_OVERLOADED)
into a cosmetic capability byte in the emitted JSON (lib/job/job.c).  The
request was then enqueued on the same 4-slot pool regardless, so the flag
gave no actual control over the lockup it was meant to address.

Replace the dead guard with real admission control.  Use
lws_threadpool_diagnose() to read the live pool state and, when there is
no free worker (ongoing >= possible), reject the blame request with 503
Service Unavailable rather than enqueueing it behind work that cannot
drain.  A pool that still has a free worker keeps admitting requests, so
this only sheds load once the pool is genuinely saturated.

This bounds the number of blame tasks competing for the single lws
service thread's SYNC round-trips, which is the root cause of the
deadlock under crawl.

The cosmetic flag is kept (and still set under contention) so pages that
render a "blame may be slow" notice continue to do so.
---
 src/protocol_gitohashi.c |   44 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 42 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/protocol_gitohashi.c b/src/protocol_gitohashi.c
index 6a6519b..622a623 100644
--- a/src/protocol_gitohashi.c
+++ b/src/protocol_gitohashi.c
@@ -568,13 +568,53 @@ callback_gitohashi(struct lws *wsi, enum lws_callback_reasons reason,
 			return -1;
 		}
 
-		if (strstr(priv->url, "/blame")) {
+		/*
+		 * Blame is expensive (a full git_blame_file plus per-hunk
+		 * commit lookups) and every buffer it produces needs a
+		 * serialized round-trip through the single lws service thread
+		 * via LWS_TP_RETURN_SYNC.  Under a crawl, blame requests pile
+		 * up faster than they drain: every worker ends up parked in
+		 * the sync wait, the queue fills, and the pool deadlocks.
+		 *
+		 * When the pool is already busy with blame-class work, shed
+		 * the new request with 503 instead of admitting it onto the
+		 * same jammed pool.  Shedding only happens when there is no
+		 * free worker and no queue headroom left, so a pool that can
+		 * still make progress keeps admitting requests.
+		 */
+
+		if (vhd->tp && strstr(priv->url, "/blame")) {
 			int ongoing, possible, queue_depth;
 
-			lws_threadpool_diagnose(vhd->tp, &ongoing, &possible, &queue_depth);
+			lws_threadpool_diagnose(vhd->tp, &ongoing,
+						&possible, &queue_depth);
 
+			/* park the flag so the page can render a notice if
+			 * we do admit a request that later sees contention */
 			if (ongoing >= 1 || queue_depth)
 				priv->blame_overloaded = 1;
+
+			/*
+			 * no free worker AND no queue slot left... the pool
+			 * cannot accept another long blame without joining
+			 * the deadlock.  Reject this one immediately.
+			 */
+			if (ongoing >= possible) {
+				lwsl_notice("%s: blame shed: ongoing %d/%d, "
+					    "q %d\n", __func__, ongoing,
+					    possible, queue_depth);
+
+				lws_return_http_status(wsi,
+					HTTP_STATUS_SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE,
+					"503 Blame pool busy");
+
+				cleanup_task_private_data(wsi, priv);
+
+				if (lws_http_transaction_completed(wsi))
+					return -1;
+
+				return 0;
+			}
 		}
 
 		if (!lws_threadpool_enqueue(vhd->tp, &targs, "goh-%s",
